WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.715
A cloud GPU is accelerator capacity rented from a provider and attached

00:00:04.715 --> 00:00:07.792
to a remote computing instance or managed service.

00:00:07.792 --> 00:00:11.254
Instead of purchasing, installing, and maintaining a graphics processor,

00:00:11.254 --> 00:00:16.254
a user selects an available GPU configuration, starts it in a provider region,

00:00:16.254 --> 00:00:18.562
and pays for the resources consumed.

00:00:18.562 --> 00:00:22.408
The instance normally includes virtual CPUs, system memory, storage, networking,

00:00:22.408 --> 00:00:27.408
an operating system image, and access controls in addition to the GPU itself.

00:00:27.408 --> 00:00:29.715
Cloud GPUs support several workload types.

00:00:29.715 --> 00:00:34.715
Training and fine-tuning update model weights and can require long runs, high memory,

00:00:34.715 --> 00:00:35.869
and multiple accelerators.

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:38.946
Inference loads a trained model to answer requests.

00:00:38.946 --> 00:00:43.562
Batch jobs process queued documents, images, or data without an interactive user.

00:00:43.562 --> 00:00:49.715
Experimentation may need a powerful GPU for only a few hours to test compatibility or performance.

00:00:49.715 --> 00:00:53.562
Each pattern has different requirements for uptime, storage, network transfer,

00:00:53.562 --> 00:00:54.715
and interruption tolerance.

00:00:54.715 --> 00:00:58.562
Local hardware offers direct control and predictable availability after purchase,

00:00:58.562 --> 00:01:02.408
but it requires capital, physical space, power, cooling, and maintenance.

00:01:02.408 --> 00:01:07.023
Cloud capacity replaces much of that initial capital cost with operational spending.

00:01:07.023 --> 00:01:11.254
It can be provisioned quickly and released when the work ends,

00:01:11.254 --> 00:01:14.331
but the meter continues while resources remain allocated.

00:01:14.331 --> 00:01:18.946
Compute is only one charge; persistent disks, snapshots, object storage, public addresses,

00:01:18.946 --> 00:01:21.254
and data transfer may continue independently.

00:01:21.254 --> 00:01:25.100
Cloud GPUs are therefore not simply someone else's graphics card.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.715
They are a temporary service embedded in a larger cloud control plane.

00:01:29.715 --> 00:01:34.331
Good use depends on selecting the right accelerator, securing the surrounding resources,

00:01:34.331 --> 00:01:35.869
measuring performance, protecting data,

00:01:35.869 --> 00:01:40.100
and ending or downsizing the environment when its purpose is complete.
